.\" Generated by "sysml -man"; edit the description in the command's usage.go.
.TH SYSML 1 "2026-08-30" "OpenSysML" "OpenSysML Manual"
.SH NAME
sysml \- run, check, convert and render SysML v2 and KerML models
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysml
[options] [file...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
sysml loads the models it is given \(em a file, a directory to walk or a glob
\(em as a single model, so a declaration in one file resolves against the
others whichever order they were named in. With no expression or check to
carry out it opens an interactive prompt; otherwise it does what was asked and
exits on the verdict, which is what lets a run gate a build.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help
Show this help and exit
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-version
Show the version and exit
.TP
.B \-man
Write this command's manual page, in roff, to stdout and exit
.SS Evaluating
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-eval " \fIexpr\fP"
Evaluate this expression, against the model when one is loaded, and exit
(repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-query " \fItext\fP"
Evaluate this OSLC Query text against the model and exit
.SS Checking a model
.TP
.BR \-validate "[=\fIobject\fP]"
Report the model's diagnostics and exit, nonzero on an error;
\-validate=<object> checks instead every assertion about that object
(repeatable)
.TP
.B \-strict
Judge the model as conforming SysML v2: notation no pinned production admits
is an error, not a warning
.TP
.BR \-constraint " \fIname\fP"
Evaluate this constraint and exit (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-requirement " \fIname\fP"
Evaluate this requirement, and every verification case verifying it, and exit
(repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-satisfy "[=\fIname\fP]"
Evaluate every satisfaction assertion, or with \-satisfy=<name> those the
named element states, and exit (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-calc " \fIcall\fP"
Invoke this calculation and report its result, as \-calc "Fall(3, 4)"
(repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-analysis " \fIcall\fP"
Run this analysis or verification case and report its outputs and verdict, as
\-analysis "Pkg::Case(3.0) Pkg::part" (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-record\-run " \fIcall\fP"
Run this analysis case as \-analysis does and record the run into the model as
AnalysisRecords elements, one record per \-sweep value or \-runs run
(repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-record\-into " \fIpackage\fP"
Record \-record\-run runs into this package instead of a Records package
beside the case's
.TP
.BR \-run\-query " \fIquery\fP"
Execute this document query and report its rows, as \-run\-query "Heavy
root=telescope" (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-instantiate " \fIname\fP"
Create an object of this definition or usage before the checks, so a verdict
is about it (repeatable)
.TP
.B \-json
Report checks as one JSON document rather than as lines
.SS Running behaviors
.TP
.BR \-action " \fIname\fP"
Run this action to completion, as \-action "Drive rover1" to run it on an
object (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-state " \fIname\fP"
Run this state machine, as \-state "Mission rover1" to run it on an object
(repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-advance " \fItime\fP"
Simulated time units to run the \-action and \-state behaviors for, on one
shared clock; without it a state machine takes only its initial transition
.TP
.BR \-schedule " \fIpolicy\fP"
Policy every run resolves its choice points under: declared, reverse
(default), seed:<n>, explore[:runs=N,depth=D] or replay:<file>
.TP
.BR \-seed " \fIn\fP"
Seed the model's own draws \(em weighted decisions, random functions \(em and
those of \-samples and \-runs; the same seed draws the same run or table
.TP
.BR \-draws " \fIpolicy\fP"
How every run resolves the draws of RandomFunctions: random (default) draws
from \-seed; min, max and average take each call's least, greatest or mean
value and need no seed; weighted decisions draw from \-seed whatever the
policy
.TP
.BR \-clock\-step " \fIseconds\fP"
The step, in seconds, the clock of every run ticks by: a wait comes due at the
first multiple of it not before the wait ends; 0 (default) is a continuous
clock. With \-compare\-results, replaces every configuration's stepSize
.TP
.BR \-runs " \fIn\fP"
Run the \-action, or the Simulation::MonteCarlo \-analysis, this many times,
each run seeded from \-seed, and table the \-observe features or the case's
observed value; needs \-seed unless \-draws is min, max or average
.TP
.BR \-observe " \fIfeature\fP"
Report this feature of the \-runs action, or clock for the time it completed
at; default every feature it holds and the clock. With \-compare\-results, the
stored observable to compare, or <observable>=<feature> to read it from
another feature of the run (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-sweep " \fIrange\fP"
Run the \-analysis or \-calc once per value of this range, as \-sweep
"n=1..8:2"; several ranges run their cartesian product (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-samples " \fIn\fP"
Draw this many values uniformly from each \-sweep range instead of stepping
through it; needs \-seed
.TP
.BR \-jobs " \fIn\fP"
Runs of one check that may go concurrently, each on a worker of its own;
default OPENSYSML_JOBS, else one per CPU, fewer where the memory available
leaves less than 512 MiB per worker
.SS Analysis engines
.TP
.B \-engines
List the analysis engines this build knows and their status, spawning nothing,
and exit
.TP
.B \-probe
With \-engines, also start each external engine once and report the outcome as
its status
.TP
.BR \-engine " \fIname\fP"
Analysis engine every check is put to: auto (default), all, or one by name
\(em run, explore, check, smt, sweep, solve, tool:<name> or an external engine
.SS Checking every schedule (\-engine check, smt or all)
.TP
.BR \-check\-property " \fIname\fP"
Evaluate this constraint or requirement at every stable state of the action
and report a schedule at which it is false (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-check\-diverge " \fIfeature\fP"
Report this feature sensitive when schedules leave it with different final
values, as x, step.out or this.level; default every attribute (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-check\-input " \fIfeature\fP"
Under smt, leave this feature of the action free in its declared domain
although the model binds it (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-check\-assume " \fIname\fP"
Under smt, assume this constraint or requirement over the initial state of the
action (repeatable)
.TP
.BR \-check\-witness " \fIdir\fP"
Write a witness file for each violation and each divergent value to this
directory, for \-schedule replay:<file> and %replay to follow
.TP
.BR \-check\-depth " \fIn\fP"
The most moves one schedule may make before the search backtracks, or the
moves smt unrolls the action to (default 10000 for check, 40 for smt)
.TP
.BR \-check\-states " \fIn\fP"
Under check, the most distinct states the search may visit (default 1000000)
.TP
.BR \-check\-unroll " \fIn\fP"
Under smt, the most iterations of one loop the solver unrolls before it stops
(default 4)
.TP
.BR \-check\-timeout " \fIduration\fP"
The time the check's plan may run for, as 30s or 2m, and the time each smt
solver query may take in place of OPENSYSML_SMT_TIMEOUT
.SS Converting and migrating
.TP
.BR \-convert " \fIformat\fP"
Convert the model to this format instead of running it: sysml, kerml, ttl,
turtle, rdf or api\-json (RDF and the API element form are experimental). The
input may be a Flexo branch URL (host[:port][/base]/projects/{p}/branches/{b}
of the FLEXO_SYSMLV2_URL endpoint, or flexo://{p}/{b}), read as its RDF graph
.TP
.BR \-from " \fIformat\fP"
Input format for \-convert: sysml, kerml, ttl, turtle, rdf, api\-json, or xmi,
uml or mdzip for a SysML v1 model to migrate (experimental); default the
input's extension
.TP
.BR \-id " \fIname\fP"
With \-convert ttl or api\-json, how derived element ids are spelled:
qualified (default) derives each from its qualified name; uuid mints
name\-based uuids under each root package, as the library convention does
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-output " \fIfile\fP"
Write what \-convert, \-compile, \-render or \-render\-document produces to
this file instead of stdout; with \-convert ttl, a Flexo branch URL pushes the
graph to the branch
.TP
.BR \-migration\-report " \fIfile\fP"
With \-convert from xmi, write the element\-by\-element migration report to
this file: JSON when it ends in .json, text otherwise
.TP
.BR \-migration\-results " \fIfile\fP"
With \-convert from xmi, write the run configurations and the result snapshots
the simulation tool stored for them to this JSON file, for \-compare\-results
to read against the migrated model
.TP
.BR \-layout " \fIfile\fP"
With \-convert from xmi, read this MTIP export (HUDS XML) and write the
diagram geometry it records as DiagramLayout annotations in the migrated views
.TP
.BR \-image\-base\-url " \fIurl\fP"
With \-convert from xmi, the http(s) URL a comment's relative <img src> is
resolved against, such as the View Editor server
.TP
.BR \-compare\-results " \fIfile\fP"
Run every configuration this \-migration\-results file indexes \(em or those
\-action names \(em with its recorded runs and duration mode, or the \-runs
and \-draws given, seeded from \-seed, and table the tool's and OpenSysML's
min, mean, p50, p90 and max of each observable with their relative difference
.SS Compiling natively
.TP
.BR \-compile " \fIcalc\fP"
Compile this calc def to a native executable named by \-o, as \-compile
Pkg::Fib
.TP
.BR \-target " \fIbackend\fP"
Backend \-compile generates code for: c or go. The default is c.
.TP
.B \-source
With \-compile, write the generated source to \-o instead of building it
.SS Rendering views
.TP
.BR \-render " \fIview\fP"
Render this view of the model instead of running it, in the form its render
member states
.TP
.BR \-render\-all " \fIdir\fP"
Render every declared view into this directory
.TP
.BR \-render\-form " \fIform\fP"
Form \-render or \-render\-all writes: text, mermaid, markdown, dot or
plantuml; default from the destination for \-render, each kind's machine form
for \-render\-all
.TP
.BR \-render\-palette " \fIpalette\fP"
Palette the dot or plantuml form fills nodes from, by keyword family:
okabe\-ito, tol\-bright, tol\-muted, tol\-light, brewer\-set2, brewer\-dark2,
viridis or cividis; default black and white
.TP
.BR \-render\-unplaced " \fIplacement\fP"
Where a graph form of a view some Layout positions puts the nodes none does:
omit (default) leaves them undrawn in every form, strip draws them, in rows
below the dot drawing; applies to \-render, \-render\-all and document
diagrams
.TP
.BR \-render\-style " \fIstyle\fP"
Drawing style of the dot form: pilot (default), the Pilot visualizer's black
and white, or cameo, the look of Cameo Systems Modeler; applies to \-render,
\-render\-all and document diagrams
.SS Rendering documents
.TP
.BR \-render\-document " \fIname\fP"
Compile this document definition, run its queries and write the rendered
document
.TP
.BR \-render\-documents " \fIdir\fP"
Render every document definition, linked to one another, into this directory;
a document that cannot be rendered gets a page stating why and the run exits 3
.TP
.BR \-doc\-form " \fIform\fP"
Form the documents are written in: markdown (default), html or pdf, which
drives an external converter
.TP
.BR \-diagram\-form " \fIform\fP"
Form the documents' graph\-shaped diagrams are written in: mermaid, dot or
plantuml; unset, a positioned view is dot and any other mermaid; a table\-kind
view is a table either way
.TP
.B \-doc\-title\-page
Put the document title on a page of its own (html or pdf)
.TP
.B \-doc\-toc
Write a table of contents ahead of the content (html or pdf)
.TP
.B \-doc\-number\-sections
Number the section headings hierarchically (html or pdf)
.TP
.BR \-pdf\-engine " \fIconverter\fP"
Converter \-doc\-form pdf drives: weasyprint (default), pandoc or prince
.SS Styling HTML documents
.TP
.BR \-html\-theme " \fItheme\fP"
Style the HTML page or PDF with a bundled theme layered over the default
stylesheet: default, acm, ieee, modern, nasa, print or report
.TP
.BR \-html\-css " \fIfile|url\fP"
Style the HTML or PDF with this stylesheet too: a file is inlined, a URL is
linked (repeatable, applied in order after the default sheet)
.TP
.B \-html\-no\-default\-css
Leave the default stylesheet out, so only \-html\-css sheets style the HTML or
PDF
.TP
.B \-html\-fragment
Write the document element alone, without the page shell or a stylesheet, to
embed in a page of your own
.TP
.B \-html\-default\-css
Write the default document stylesheet, or with \-html\-theme that theme's
whole sheet, and exit
.TP
.BR \-html\-mermaid " cdn|\fIurl\fP"
Have the page load Mermaid to draw its diagrams: cdn loads a pinned release
from jsDelivr, a URL the script it names
.TP
.BR \-html\-math " cdn|\fIurl\fP"
Have the page load MathJax to typeset its formulas: cdn loads a pinned release
from jsDelivr, a URL the script it names
.SS Syncing against a repository
.TP
.BR \-sync\-diff " \fIrepo\fP"
Show the change set between the model and this repository \(em a graph file
(.ttl) or a SysML v2 API endpoint URL \(em and exit; never writes
.TP
.BR \-sync\-apply " \fIurl\fP"
Apply the change set to the model's project branch at this SysML v2 API
endpoint URL, then record the commit in the sync state
.TP
.BR \-sync\-base " \fIfile\fP"
Repository graph at the last\-seen commit; with it, repository changes since
then surface as conflicts
.TP
.BR \-sync\-state " \fIfile\fP"
Sync state file recording project, branch and last\-seen commit; default
<model>.sync.json beside the model
.TP
.B \-sync\-confirm\-deletes
Confirm repository\-side deletes; without it the diff reports them but
applying is refused
.TP
.B \-sync\-mint\-ids
Mint a UUID for each unannotated element being created, so the repository can
address it stably
.TP
.BR \-sync\-annotate " \fIfile\fP"
Write the model to this file with each minted id declared as an @ElementId
annotation (needs \-sync\-mint\-ids)
.SS Diagnostics and profiling
.TP
.B \-debug
Report every diagnostic over the whole session buffer, with the pass that
produced it
.TP
.B \-quiet
Report errors only, suppressing warnings
.TP
.B \-trace
Report each execution step: expression evaluation, calc invocation, action
tokens, state transitions
.TP
.BR \-cpuprofile " \fIfile\fP"
Write a CPU profile of the run to this file, for go tool pprof
.TP
.BR \-memprofile " \fIfile\fP"
Write a heap profile of the run to this file, for go tool pprof
.TP
.B \-memstats
Report on stderr what the run cost: wall time, memory allocated, memory taken
from the OS
.SS Deprecated
.TP
.BR \-to " \fIformat\fP"
Replaced by \-convert, which names the output format
.TP
.B \-pdf\-title\-page
Former name of \-doc\-title\-page, which also shapes HTML
.TP
.B \-pdf\-toc
Former name of \-doc\-toc, which also shapes HTML
.TP
.B \-pdf\-number\-sections
Former name of \-doc\-number\-sections, which also shapes HTML
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 2
.nf
sysml                       # Start interactive REPL
sysml \-e "5 + 3"            # Evaluate and exit
sysml \-e "expr" file.sysml  # Load file, evaluate, and exit
sysml file.sysml            # Load file and start REPL
sysml \-debug file.sysml     # Load file, reporting every diagnostic
sysml \-trace file.sysml     # Load file, reporting each execution step
.fi
.RE
.SH CHECKING A MODEL
.RS 2
.nf
# Evaluate one constraint and exit
sysml \-constraint MassBudget model.sysml
# Evaluate one requirement and exit
sysml \-requirement PowerMargin model.sysml
# Evaluate every satisfaction assertion
sysml \-satisfy model.sysml
# \&...only the ones Ctx states
sysml \-satisfy=Ctx model.sysml
# Check C against an object of p
sysml \-instantiate p \-constraint C model.sysml
# Report diagnostics only
sysml \-validate model.sysml
# \&...asking whether it is conforming SysML v2
sysml \-validate \-strict model.sysml
# Check every assertion about an object
sysml \-instantiate car \-validate=car m.sysml
# Invoke a calculation
sysml \-calc "Fall(3, 4)" model.sysml
# Run an analysis case
sysml \-analysis shipCost model.sysml
# \&...on an object as its subject
sysml \-analysis "CostAnalysis ship" model.sysml
# Run a verification case for its verdict
sysml \-analysis speedCheck model.sysml
# Execute a document query
sysml \-run\-query "Heavy root=scope" model.sysml
# Run an action to completion
sysml \-action Drive model.sysml
# Run a state machine for 10 time units
sysml \-state Mission \-advance 10 model.sysml
# \&...an action and a machine on one clock
sysml \-action Ping \-state Sm \-advance 5 m.sysml
# Run every linearization; table the outcomes
sysml \-schedule explore \-action Drive m.sysml
# Report the verdicts as JSON
sysml \-satisfy \-json model.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each check flag may be repeated. \-validate=<object> checks every assertion
about an object \-instantiate created and the objects it holds: an object
named, #<id>, or a path such as car.engine. \-requirement and \-satisfy report
beside each verdict the verdict of every verification case verifying the
requirement. \-analysis takes arguments for the case's inputs and an object as
its subject, as \-analysis "Pkg::Case(3.0) Pkg::part"; a verification case
also reports the verdict its body produced.
.SH RUNNING ACTIONS AND STATE MACHINES
\-action runs an action to completion and \-state a state machine, both on one
shared clock that \-advance runs for the given simulated time units; without
\-advance a state machine takes only its initial transition. Either names a
definition or usage, or one followed by the object to perform it on, as
\-action "Drive rover1".
.PP
\-schedule is the policy every run resolves its choice points under \(em
concurrent tokens, overlapping guards, competing transitions: declared,
reverse (the default), seed:<n> for a reproducible pseudo\-random order,
explore[:runs=N,depth=D] to run every linearization within the budget and
table the distinct outcomes, or replay:<file> to follow a witness file's
choice lines move for move, then reverse.
.PP
Under \-schedule explore, \-engine check, smt or all, each run creates objects
of its own before the behaviors start: one per \-instantiate, which a \-state
or \-action named alone attaches to, and one per \-action or \-state naming a
definition or usage to create or a path into one such as mission.rover. The
declaration is created once per run, so machines on sibling parts share it and
its connectors.
.PP
\-jobs is how many runs of one check may go concurrently \(em the
linearizations of an exploration, the engines \-engine all consults \(em each
on a worker of its own over the shared model; the result is the same at any
count. By default one per CPU, fewer where the memory available leaves less
than 512 MiB per worker.
.SH ANALYSIS ENGINES
.RS 2
.nf
# List engines, kind and status; nothing runs
sysml \-engines
# Also start each external engine once
sysml \-engines \-probe
# The same as \-schedule explore
sysml \-engine explore \-action Drive m.sysml
# Every engine that covers the question
sysml \-engine all \-requirement R m.sysml
# One engine; the plan in the report
sysml \-engine run \-json \-constraint C m.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every check is a question put to an analysis engine: run executes the model
once under \-schedule, explore runs every linearization, sweep runs the rows
of \-sweep, and solve puts condition sets to an SMT solver. \-engine auto (the
default) picks the engine of highest authority that covers the question and
falls back to the next when it refuses or covers nothing; \-engine <name> puts
the question to that engine alone, and its refusal is the answer; \-engine all
puts it to every engine that covers it, one after another in name order, and
composes their answers: a witnessed violation stands over any universal claim,
and a contradiction is reported as a disagreement decided in the interpreter's
favor.
.PP
Every verdict is followed by its standing: the claim, the strength of the
evidence \(em observed (one run), witnessed (a replayed execution), bounded
(every case within a budget), proved (every case) \(em and what earned it, as
`holds (observed: 1 run under reverse)`. A budget the engine reached lowers
the strength and is named. Under \-json each check carries the plan and one
results[] entry per engine that answered, with its engine, claim, strength,
bounds and witness.
.PP
\-engines lists each engine's name, kind (built\-in, tool, engine, policy,
sampler), protocol, authority, the questions it answers and whether its
process is found, with the manifest entry and resolved command of each
external one; \-probe also starts each external engine once and checks its
describe against its manifest entry field by field. An engine is named run,
explore, check, smt, sweep, solve, tool:<name> from OPENSYSML_TOOLS, or as an
external engine from OPENSYSML_ENGINES. \-engine explore is \-schedule
explore; \-engine check searches every schedule of each \-action for a
violation, deadlock, failure or divergence; \-engine smt decides each
\-check\-property over every schedule and every value of the action's free
inputs through an SMT solver.
.SH CHECKING EVERY SCHEDULE
.RS 2
.nf
# Search every schedule for Safe
sysml \-engine check \-action Go \-check\-property Safe m.sysml
# Is speed schedule\-dependent?
sysml \-engine check \-action Go \-check\-diverge speed m.sysml
sysml \-engine smt \-action Go \-check\-input t \-check\-property Safe \e
    m.sysml
# A witness file per finding
sysml \-engine check \-action Go \-check\-witness out m.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
\-check\-property evaluates a constraint or requirement at every stable state
of the action, on the performing object when there is one, and reports a
schedule at which it is false. \-check\-diverge reports a feature sensitive
when schedules leave it with different final values \(em check searching the
schedules, smt asking the solver for two that end it apart \(em named as x,
step.out for a performed node's output or this.level for the performing
object's (not covered under smt); a name nothing holds is refused, and without
it every attribute of the action and of its performing object is checked, the
action's own when it has none.
.PP
Under smt every input the model leaves unbound is free and every bound one is
pinned; \-check\-input leaves a bound feature free in its declared domain, and
a name that is not a feature the action reads is refused. \-check\-assume
assumes a constraint or requirement over the initial state; a set no initial
state satisfies is reported not covered, never proved.
.PP
\-check\-witness writes a file for each violation and each divergent value
\(em the inputs the solver chose, the schedule's choices, a blank line, then
the run's trace \(em which \-schedule replay:<file> and %replay follow. A
bound is named when it is hit: \-check\-depth is the most moves one schedule
may make before the search backtracks, or the moves smt unrolls the action to;
\-check\-states the most distinct states the search may visit, the same figure
\-engine all gives an exploration as its runs; \-check\-unroll the most
iterations of one loop smt unrolls; \-check\-timeout the time the plan may run
for and the time each solver query may take in place of OPENSYSML_SMT_TIMEOUT
\(em a search it stops is reported incomplete with the states and depth
reached, not as a verdict.
.SH SWEEPING AND SAMPLING A PARAMETER
.RS 2
.nf
# One run per range value
sysml \-calc Twice \-sweep "n=1..8:2" m.sysml
# Every combination of the two
sysml \-analysis C \-sweep "m=1..3" \-sweep "v=1..2" m.sysml
# 20 values drawn uniformly
sysml \-analysis C \-sweep "m=1..9" \-samples 20 \-seed 7 m.sysml
# Report the rows as JSON
sysml \-analysis C \-sweep "m=1..3" \-json m.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
A sweep is orchestration over the ordinary run: each row is one \-analysis or
\-calc invocation with the swept parameter bound to that row's value and every
other argument as given, so nothing about how a case executes changes. Name a
single \-analysis or \-calc; a parameter the case does not declare, one the
invocation already binds, a step of zero or one pointing away from the end of
the range is an error rather than a silent row.
.PP
An endpoint and a step carry the syntax and the units an argument carries
("mass=0.0 [SI::kg]..9.0 [SI::kg]:3.0 [SI::kg]"), and the end of the range is
included where the step lands on it.
.PP
The values are produced in the swept parameter's declared type, not the
literals': a Real parameter swept over 1..4:1 is bound to 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0
and an Integer one swept over 1.0..3.0:1.0 to 1, 2, 3, while an endpoint or
step no Integer parameter can take (:0.5) is refused before any run, as is a
range over a Boolean, String, enumeration or non\-scalar parameter. A
parameter declaring no type takes the range as written, and the table says so.
.PP
A range between whole numbers steps by one where no :<step> is written; one
with a fractional endpoint needs one. A range read as reals takes an Integer
endpoint or step only where a Real holds it without rounding, and steps only
where the reals tell its rows apart. Rows come out in the order the ranges
were given, the first varying slowest, and a run that failed is a row carrying
its error rather than the end of the table.
.PP
\-samples draws that many values uniformly from each range instead of stepping
through it \(em Integers inclusively for a parameter taking Integers, reals in
[<from>, <to>) for one taking reals \(em and needs \-seed: the same seed draws
the same table on every platform. Uniform is the only distribution on offer
\(em the bundled library states no probability distributions \(em so a range
must be written <from>..<to> rather than as a named distribution.
.SH RUNNING AN ACTION MANY TIMES
.RS 2
.nf
# 100 runs, every feature
sysml \-action Acquire \-runs 100 \-seed 7 m.sysml
# One observable
sysml \-action Acquire \-runs 100 \-seed 7 \-observe elapsed m.sysml
# One run, its draws seeded
sysml \-action Acquire \-seed 7 m.sysml
# Durations at their max
sysml \-action Acquire \-draws max m.sysml
# Mean durations
sysml \-action A \-runs 9 \-seed 7 \-draws average m.sysml
# A MonteCarlo analysis case
sysml \-analysis "Mc obj" \-runs 100 \-seed 7 m.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
\-runs runs one \-action to completion that many times, each run on a fresh
context whose modeled randomness \(em the weighted decisions @Probability
states and the draws of uniform, uniformInteger, triangular and normal \(em is
seeded from a seed of its own derived from \-seed, so the same seed makes the
same table on every platform and a run can be replayed alone. \-schedule stays
the second, independent knob: it decides the concurrency choices, which carry
no probability, in every run alike.
.PP
\-draws is the third knob, the duration policy: random (the default) draws
every RandomFunctions call from the seed; min, max and average resolve each
call to the least, greatest or mean value of its distribution instead \(em the
midpoint of a uniform, the mean of a triangular \(em so a run whose only
randomness is durations completes deterministically and needs no \-seed.
normal has no least or greatest value, so a run that calls it under min or max
stops with an error. Weighted decisions are not durations: they draw from the
seed under every policy, and an unseeded run takes the most probable branch.
The policy is written into every witness as `draws by <policy>`, and
\-schedule replay: follows it.
.PP
The table has one row per run, numbered, with each \-observe feature of the
action \(em `clock` is the simulation time the run completed at, never a
feature of that name \(em and without \-observe every feature the action holds
and the clock. Below it each numeric observable is summarised over the
completed runs: min, mean, max, the nearest\-rank p50 and p90, and a
histogram; a non\-numeric one is counted by value. A feature the action does
not hold is refused.
.PP
\-runs also runs an \-analysis that specializes Simulation::MonteCarlo, the
OpenSysML library's analysis of repeated runs: each run performs the case's
steps on a fresh subject, seeded as an action's run is, and reads the value
the case binds as observed; the table has one row per run with that value, and
beneath its distribution the case is concluded once over the sample \(em runs,
mean, deviation (the sample standard deviation) and outOfSpec (the runs in
which a check of the case did not hold) bound, and the case's own outputs and
checks evaluated over them. Each run makes its objects from their
declarations, so a subject named by `#id` alone is refused. Run once, without
\-runs, such a case leaves its statistics unbound. \-observe belongs to an
action's runs, not a case's.
.SH CONVERSION
.RS 2
.nf
# SysML notation to RDF Turtle, on stdout
sysml model.sysml \-convert ttl
# RDF Turtle to SysML notation
sysml model.ttl \-convert sysml
# Write the conversion to a file
sysml model.sysml \-convert ttl \-o m.ttl
# Name the input format explicitly
sysml in.txt \-convert ttl \-from sysml
# A Flexo branch as notation
sysml flexo://demo/main \-convert sysml
# Push the graph to the branch
sysml model.sysml \-convert ttl \-o flexo://demo/main
.fi
.RE
.PP
The input format is taken from the file extension (.sysml, .kerml, .ttl,
\&.json) unless \-from names it: sysml, kerml, ttl, turtle, rdf, api\-json, or
xmi, uml or mdzip for a SysML v1 model to migrate, whose element\-by\-element
report \-migration\-report writes out; \-layout names an MTIP export of the
same project, whose diagram geometry is written into the migrated views as
DiagramLayout metadata. Converting to the format it is already in rewrites the
input: notation is reformatted, Turtle is normalized.
.PP
Either side may name a Flexo MMS project branch instead of a file:
http(s)://host[:port][/base]/projects/{project}/branches/{branch}, or
flexo://{project}/{branch}, both naming the endpoint FLEXO_SYSMLV2_URL
configures. A branch input is read as its head commit's RDF graph; a branch
\-o takes the \-convert ttl output as the branch's whole model graph,
conditional on the branch's etag, and refuses a head the sync state says has
moved. Both need the bearer token in FLEXO_INTEROP_TOKEN and record the head
commit in the sync state (\-sync\-state, or <output>.sync.json on a read /
<model>.sync.json on a push).
.PP
RDF conversion \(em Turtle and the API's JSON element form alike \(em is
experimental: the mapping covers model structure and the behavior its bodies
state, refuses what it cannot write back, and its vocabulary may change
without a compatibility path; see docs/reference/rdf\-mapping.md \(sc Status
.PP
SysML v1 migration is experimental: the mapping covers structure, ports and
connectors, requirements, constraints, instances and allocations, reports
every element it approximates or leaves behind, and what it writes for a v1
element may change without a compatibility path; see
docs/reference/sysml\-v1\-migration.md \(sc Status
.PP
Every run that converts RDF or migrates a v1 model says so on stderr. Saving
to .sysml or .kerml is stable.
.SH NATIVE COMPILATION
.RS 2
.nf
# Compile a calc def to a C executable
sysml model.sysml \-compile Pkg::Fib \-o fib
# \&...via Go
sysml model.sysml \-compile Pkg::Fib \-target go \-o fib
# Write the generated source only
sysml model.sysml \-compile Pkg::Fib \-source \-o fib.c
.fi
.RE
.PP
The executable takes the calc's parameters as arguments and prints its result;
it computes what sysml \-calc computes, or fails with the same error. Only the
scalar subset compiles (Integer, Real, Boolean; see
docs/project/native\-compilation.md).
.SH SYNCING AGAINST A REPOSITORY
A dry run unless \-sync\-apply:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
# Show the change set and exit
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-diff repo.ttl
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-diff repo.ttl \-sync\-base last\-seen.ttl
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-diff repo.ttl \-sync\-confirm\-deletes
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-diff repo.ttl \-sync\-mint\-ids \-sync\-annotate \e
    out.sysml
# Against the live API; no writes
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-diff http://localhost:8083
# Write the change set as a commit
sysml model.sysml \-sync\-apply http://localhost:8083
.fi
.RE
.PP
The diff correlates elements by their effective id \(em an @ElementId
annotation, or the encoded qualified name \(em so a rename, move or retype is
an update, never a delete plus a create. Repository\-only elements are
reported as deletes but applying them needs \-sync\-confirm\-deletes;
conflicts \(em a declared id the branch no longer has, or a repository change
since the last\-seen commit \(em exit 1 and are never resolved silently.
.PP
The last\-seen commit is tool state in <model>.sync.json (or \-sync\-state),
never written into the notation. \-sync\-apply refuses a change set the dry
run would have flagged, sends each update under its retained id, and records
the resulting commit; the token comes from FLEXO_INTEROP_TOKEN.
.SH RENDERING A VIEW
.RS 2
.nf
# ASCII text at a terminal
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form markdown
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-o view.mmd
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form dot
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form dot \e
    \-render\-palette okabe\-ito
# unpositioned nodes in a strip below
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form dot \e
    \-render\-unplaced strip
# drawn as Cameo draws it
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form dot \e
    \-render\-style cameo
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form plantuml \-o \e
    view.puml
# several files, loaded as one model
sysml types.sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView
sysml model.sysml \-render\-all rendered
.fi
.RE
.PP
The rendering is the one the view's render member states, and a containment
tree where it states none. It is tool\-defined output: SysML v2 specifies the
notation, not how a tool draws it. Notices \(em an empty view, an element the
rendering cannot represent \(em go on stderr. Every file named is loaded as
one model, so a view may expose elements a sibling file declares.
\-render\-form names the form written \(em text, mermaid, markdown, dot or
plantuml; by default \-render takes it from the destination and \-render\-all
writes each kind's machine form, one file per view named by its qualified name
with :: as . and every byte unsafe in a filename (/, \e, :, ., %, control
characters, what Windows reserves) as %XX; a name past 255 bytes is cut and
tagged ~ and a hash of the whole. A graph\-shaped rendering is written as a
Mermaid diagram by default, as Graphviz DOT with \-render\-form dot and as
PlantUML with \-render\-form plantuml, which also writes a sequence rendering;
neither Graphviz nor PlantUML is needed to write them. Both are drawn in the
black\-and\-white style of the SysML v2 Pilot visualizer; \-render\-palette
fills their nodes by keyword family from a colourblind\-safe palette
(okabe\-ito, tol\-bright, tol\-muted, tol\-light, brewer\-set2, brewer\-dark2,
viridis or cividis), keeping black text legible on every fill. \-render\-style
names the look the DOT form draws in: pilot (default), the Pilot visualizer's,
or cameo, the look of Cameo Systems Modeler \(em a diagram frame with a header
tab, Arial text, gradient fills, compartments and the UML pseudo\-state
symbols \(em for a diagram migrated from Cameo to keep its look. A
DiagramLayout Style on a member colours it over either look, and a Note is
drawn beside the member it is about. A view whose members carry DiagramLayout
positions draws the placed members and the edges between them in every graph
form, and leaves a member with no position undrawn: the DOT form pins each at
its stated place, so nothing lands on a positioned box, while Mermaid and
PlantUML lay the same members out themselves. \-render\-unplaced strip draws
the unplaced members too, in rows in a strip below a DOT drawing. The same
setting shapes the diagrams of \-render\-document and \-render\-documents.
.SH RENDERING A DOCUMENT
.RS 2
.nf
# Markdown on stdout
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-o report.md
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form pdf \e
    \-o report.pdf
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form html \e
    \-o report.html
# every document, linked
sysml model.sysml \-render\-documents rendered
sysml model.sysml \-render\-documents site \-doc\-form html \-html\-css \e
    theme.css
# a bundled theme
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form html \e
    \-html\-theme report \-o report.html
# diagrams drawn in the browser
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form html \e
    \-html\-mermaid cdn \-o report.html
# formulas typeset in the browser
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form html \e
    \-html\-math cdn \-o report.html
# diagrams as Graphviz DOT
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-diagram\-form \e
    dot \-o report.md
# diagrams as PlantUML
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-diagram\-form \e
    plantuml \-o report.md
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form pdf \e
    \-pdf\-engine pandoc \-doc\-title\-page \-doc\-toc \-doc\-number\-sections \e
    \-o report.pdf
# the default stylesheet
sysml \-html\-default\-css \-o sysml\-document.css
# a theme's full sheet
sysml \-html\-default\-css \-html\-theme modern \-o modern.css
.fi
.RE
.PP
A document is a part def specializing DocumentQueries::Document. Its queries
are bound in the model and run against it, and the result is written as
CommonMark\-compatible Markdown. Its diagram blocks are chosen per diagram: a
view some DiagramLayout::Layout or Route positions is drawn by Graphviz where
it states, as inline SVG when dot is installed and as a dot fence otherwise,
and every other graph\-shaped view is Mermaid source; with Graphviz absent a
positioned view falls back to Mermaid under a notice saying so.
\-diagram\-form mermaid, dot or plantuml writes every graph\-shaped one in
that form instead, in Markdown and HTML alike, while a table\-kind view stays
a table. Neither Graphviz nor PlantUML is needed to write a fence.
.PP
\-doc\-form html writes semantic HTML instead, carrying each element's
identity and kind, styled by a stylesheet in a cascade layer your own CSS
overrides without !important.
.PP
\-doc\-form pdf lays that HTML out with a print stylesheet through an external
converter named by \-pdf\-engine \(em weasyprint (default) or prince, or
pandoc, which reads the Markdown instead \(em run as a subprocess, never
linked in; diagrams are pre\-rendered to SVG with mermaid\-cli (mmdc).
\-html\-theme, \-html\-css and \-html\-no\-default\-css style the PDF as they
style the page. None of these tools is needed until PDF output is asked for;
scripts/download\-doc\-pdf\-toolchain.sh provisions pinned copies.
.PP
HTML output needs nothing external and loads nothing by default: \-html\-theme
picks one of the bundled looks (default, acm, ieee, modern, nasa, print,
report; acm, ieee and nasa follow those bodies' manuscript conventions),
\-html\-css adds your own stylesheets, \-html\-no\-default\-css drops the
default one, \-html\-fragment writes the document element alone to embed in a
page of yours, and \-html\-default\-css writes the default sheet out \(em or,
with \-html\-theme, a theme's whole sheet \(em to start from. \-html\-mermaid
cdn has the page load a pinned Mermaid release from jsDelivr so a browser
draws the Mermaid diagrams, or names a URL of your own to load it from.
.PP
Mathematics is LaTeX: a Span styled math is an inline formula, a Formula block
a display one. Markdown writes them between $ and $$ delimiters, HTML in \e(
\e) and \e[ \e] inside sysml\-math elements, left as source until \-html\-math
cdn has the page load a pinned MathJax release from jsDelivr, or a URL of your
own; PDF typesets them with KaTeX (katex) as it pre\-renders diagrams with
mermaid\-cli.
.SH FLAG ORDER
Flags may be written before or after the model they apply to. A file named
like a flag is read as a file after \-\-, which ends the flags: sysml \-trace
\-\- \-m.sysml
.SH READING FROM STANDARD INPUT
.RS 2
.nf
cat model.sysml | sysml \-validate \-  # A lone \- names standard input
cat model.sysml | sysml \- \-convert ttl \-from sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
What was read from standard input is called <stdin> in diagnostics, and a file
really named "\-" is read by naming it ./\- instead.
.SH PROFILING A RUN
.RS 2
.nf
# Report what the run cost, on stderr
sysml \-validate \-memstats model.sysml
# Write a heap profile for go tool pprof
sysml \-validate \-memprofile heap.out model.sysml
# Write a CPU profile for go tool pprof
sysml \-validate \-cpuprofile cpu.out model.sysml
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Every run that is not a prompt exits:
.TP
.B 0
It did what was asked.
.TP
.B 1
The model answered false for a check.
.TP
.B 2
What was asked could not be carried out at all \(em an unreadable file, a
model that did not analyse cleanly, an unresolved name, a failed conversion.
.TP
.B 3
Part of what was asked was carried out: a \-render\-documents set in which
some document could not be rendered. The others were written, a page stating
the error stands in for each that was not, and each failure is reported with
the document's qualified name.
.SH OUTPUT STREAMS
What was asked for is reported on stdout and what went wrong on stderr,
prefixed "sysml: " unless it locates a finding in the source.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_LIBRARY_PATH
Directory to load the SysML/KerML standard library from, instead of the copy
embedded in the binary.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS
Expression evaluations one run may spend before it is reported as a runaway.
Default 10000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ACTION_STEPS
Token\-flow steps one action run may perform. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_EVENTS
Events one state machine run may dispatch. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_DO_STEPS
Do actions one state machine run may perform. Default 5000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ELEMENTS
Collection elements one evaluation may hold, which bounds the memory a run
holds rather than the work it does. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_CALC_DEPTH
Nested calc invocations one run may hold on the stack, which is what a
recursion spends. Default 10000, ceiling 25000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_SWEEP_RUNS
Runs one parameter sweep or sample may make, each a whole analysis or calc run
of its own with its own budgets. Default 1000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_JOBS
Runs of one check that may go concurrently \(em the linearizations of an
exploration, the engines \-engine all consults \(em each on a worker of its
own over the shared model; \-jobs and %jobs override it. Default one per CPU,
fewer where the memory available leaves less than 512 MiB per worker.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_TOOLS
Directory of the tool manifest: one JSON file per external tool (toolName,
version, executable, variables), each registered as the engine tool:<name>
that runs actions and calcs annotated ToolExecution with that toolName. Unset
registers no tool, and such an action or calc is refused.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_TOOL_TIMEOUT
How long one tool invocation may take, as a Go duration. Default 10s, after
which the performance or calculation fails.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT
Executable the %check, %explain, %solve, %configure and %optimize commands
drive as their SMT solver, speaking SMT\-LIB2 on standard input
(experimental). Unset looks for z3, then cvc5, on PATH.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_TIMEOUT
How long one solver query may take, as a Go duration. Default 10s, after which
the verdict is unknown; a check's \-check\-timeout takes its place for the smt
engine's queries.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_CORE_BUDGET
How long %explain may spend reducing an unsat core to a minimal one, as a Go
duration. Default 30s.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_MAX_CONFIGURATIONS
How many variant selections %configure ... all may report before saying the
enumeration was cut short. Default 32.
.PP
Each variable above also answers to its legacy SYSML_\-prefixed name
(SYSML_MAX_STEPS for OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS, and so on), which remains accepted.
When both are set and the OPENSYSML_ value is non\-empty, the OPENSYSML_ value
wins; setting only the legacy name prints a one\-time deprecation warning on
standard error.
.PP
A budget bounds one run \(em one evaluation, one instantiation, one calc
invocation, one action, one state machine \(em not a whole session, so a long
session of small operations never exhausts one. A run started inside another
shares the outer run's budget.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/sysml/history
Prompt history, when XDG_STATE_HOME is set and writable.
.TP
.B ~/.sysml_history
Prompt history otherwise. When neither can be written the history is kept for
the session only.
